---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import TopicList from '../../components/overview/TopicList.astro';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../consts';

const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;

const brcd = `let config = CdlConfigBuilder::build_brcd_config()
    .with_normal_path(&normal_path)
    .with_anomalous_path(&anomalous_path)
    .with_brcd_config(BrcdConfig::<FloatType>::continuous(0))
    .with_cpdag_path(&cpdag_path)
    .build()
    .expect("Sock Shop carts_cpu_1 data files exist");

CdlBuilder::build_brcd(&config)
    .brcd_load_input()
    .brcd_discover()
    .brcd_analyze()
    .finalize()
    .print_results();`;

const brcdOut = `--- BRCD Root-Cause Ranking ---
1. [42]  posterior=1.0
2. [36]  posterior=2.0116577187047554e-98
3. [26]  posterior=1.8171175141970022e-113
4. [37]  posterior=3.8434829040437115e-254
5. [17]  posterior=3.763405292408208e-296
6. [0]  posterior=3.452356877966384e-304`;

const example = `${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/examples/causal_discovery_examples/cdl/brcd_discovery/main.rs`;
const verification = `${GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/deep_causality_algorithms/verification/brcd`;
const algorithmsReadme = `${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/deep_causality_algorithms/README.md`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Causal discovery — DeepCausality"
  description="SURD splits the information sources carry about a target into redundant, unique and synergistic parts. BRCD ranks the likely root causes of a failure. The Causal Discovery Language runs BRCD as a typed pipeline."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <article class="static-page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Overview · Causal discovery</p>
      <h1>Finding causes in data</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Two algorithms answer two questions. SURD asks how information about a target splits among
        its sources. BRCD asks which variable's mechanism changed when a system failed and ranks every
        candidate by probability.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Two questions, two algorithms</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>SURD</th><th>BRCD</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Question</td><td>How does information about a target split across its sources?</td><td>Which variable's mechanism changed?</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Input</td><td>A joint probability table of the target and its sources</td><td>Data from a normal and an anomalous period, and a causal graph</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Output</td><td>Redundant, unique and synergistic information per group of sources, plus the share no source explains</td><td>Every candidate root cause, ranked by posterior probability</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Source</td><td>Martínez-Sánchez and Lozano-Durán, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.10878">arXiv:2505.10878</a></td><td>Lee, Zhou and Kocaoglu, <a href="https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/65359">ICML 2026</a></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Both live in <code>deep_causality_algorithms</code>, next to the MRMR feature selector, which
        picks the columns most relevant to a target and least redundant with each other.
      </p>

      <h3>SURD: how information splits</h3>
      <p>
        Two sensors can each tell you something about a target, tell you the same thing, or tell you
        something only when read together; SURD measures each case. It splits the mutual information
        between a target and its sources into <em>redundant</em> information that several sources
        share, <em>unique</em> information that only one source carries, and <em>synergistic</em> information that appears only in combination. The information leak measures what no source
        explains. SURD also breaks each part down by variable state, separating contributions that
        add information about the target from those that take it away.
      </p>

      <h3>BRCD: which mechanism changed</h3>
      <p>
        When a service fails, dozens of metrics move at once. BRCD compares data from before and
        after the failure against a causal graph of the system: a supplied one, such as a service
        call map, or one the BOSS structure learner learns from the normal data. For every candidate
        root cause it scores how well the hypothesis "this variable's mechanism changed" explains the
        anomalous data, averaging over the causal graphs the data cannot tell apart, and ranks the
        candidates by posterior probability.
      </p>
      <p>
        The port reproduces the reference Python implementation's full ranking, position for
        position, on four real microservice incidents from the RCAEval benchmark, with 44 or 45
        variables each. It corrects two defects of the reference: an inverted score sign in its BOSS
        learner, and an underflow that turned its rankings into ties. It counts the causal graphs
        consistent with the data in polynomial time, using a port of the Clique-Picking algorithm of
        Wienöbst, Bannach and Liśkiewicz. The <a href={verification}>verification harnesses</a> run each of these checks.
      </p>

      <h2>The Causal Discovery Language</h2>
      <p>
        <code>deep_causality_discovery</code> runs BRCD from CSV files to a report as a typed
        pipeline. Each stage exists only on the state the stage before it produces, so the compiler
        rejects a pipeline written out of order. The compiler also checks required settings, and <code>build()</code> checks that every data file exists. The pipeline can cache a learned causal
        graph under a hash of the data and the seed, so a second run on the same data reuses it.
      </p>
      <Code code={brcd} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <Code code={brcdOut} lang="text" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The run above uses the Sock Shop incident <code>carts_cpu_1</code>: 44 service metrics and
        the supplied service-call graph. BRCD ranks shipping latency (column 42) first, then carts
        latency, user memory and catalogue latency, matching the reference implementation's ranking.
        The fault injected into that incident, carts CPU (column 0), ranks sixth in both. The <a href={example}>complete example</a> loads the data and runs the pipeline; the <a href="/examples/service-root-cause/">service root-cause example</a> walks through it.
      </p>

      <h2>From discovery to a causal model</h2>
      <p>
        Discovery and the causal engine live in separate crates, and no code converts a discovery
        result into a causal model. A SURD result guides a model's design by hand: strong unique
        information suggests a direct link from source to target, and strong synergy suggests a
        collection whose members must all hold. A BRCD ranking gives an operator a diagnosis: where
        to look first. The <a href={algorithmsReadme}>algorithms README</a> covers both algorithms in depth.
      </p>

      <TopicList current="causal-discovery" />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
